WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.994
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:34:23.864 --> 00:34:26.858
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:33.790
SETH SHOSTAK: Hi, team.

00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:40.570
Welcome to a live stream here
on Facebook Live from Google

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:43.159
in Mountain View, California--
lovely, glamorous Mountain

00:34:43.159 --> 00:34:45.679
View, California in the
heart of the Silicon Valley,

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:48.386
or if not the heart, at least
somewhere behind the ribcage.

00:34:48.386 --> 00:34:50.469
We're going to talk today
about something that you

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:53.440
probably don't think terribly
much about but should,

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:57.970
and that's the interaction
of the cloud and science.

00:34:57.970 --> 00:34:59.620
This may be the
future of science.

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:01.960
If you're doing science,
this may be your future.

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:05.414
In any case, I have with me
an expert on the Google Cloud

00:35:05.414 --> 00:35:06.830
and how it interacts
with science.

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:09.410
Let me just tell you a couple
of sentences about myself.

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:11.170
My name is Seth Shostak.

00:35:11.170 --> 00:35:14.200
And I'm a Senior Astronomer
at the SETI Institute,

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:16.460
also here in Mountain
View, California.

00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:19.400
And our job is to look for life
in space, and in particular,

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:22.050
whether there's any
intelligent life in space.

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:24.550
But we're going to talk about
that maybe a little bit later.

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:26.020
At the moment I
want to introduce

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:29.514
our expert, Massimo Mascaro.

00:35:29.514 --> 00:35:31.555
And Massimo, tell us a
little bit about yourself.

00:35:31.555 --> 00:35:32.721
MASSIMO MASCARO: Absolutely.

00:35:32.721 --> 00:35:33.970
Nice to meet everyone.

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:35.650
Yeah, my name is
Massimo Mascaro.

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:38.500
I'm a Technical
Director here at Google.

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:40.795
And actually, I work
specifically on Applied AI.

00:35:40.795 --> 00:35:42.900
I have a background in physics.

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:46.556
Actually, I was a space nerd
and an aspiring astrophysicist,

00:35:46.556 --> 00:35:48.430
but then I got into
competition neuroscience.

00:35:48.430 --> 00:35:51.460
I got [INAUDIBLE] by the
idea of neural networks

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:54.970
and I started working in AI,
and I've been doing that since.

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:56.820
Here at Google, I'm
in Google Cloud.

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:59.320
And what I do is that I work
with some of the largest Google

00:35:59.320 --> 00:36:04.030
clients, helping them applying
AI inside their problems.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:07.060
So this actually how I came
to work on this project,

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:12.220
because about in
April last year,

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:14.620
we started working with NASA,
in particular, the Frontier

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:15.670
Development Lab.

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:19.750
And at that point, actually,
my passion for space kicked in

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.427
and I decided to step
in and work with them.

00:36:22.427 --> 00:36:24.010
SETH SHOSTAK: Now,
you talk about FDL.

00:36:24.010 --> 00:36:25.960
That's the Frontier
Development Lab.

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:27.461
That's an initiative
of NASA, right?

00:36:27.461 --> 00:36:28.418
MASSIMO MASCARO: It is.

00:36:28.418 --> 00:36:30.070
SETH SHOSTAK: So how
does NASA interact

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:32.650
with the private sector,
because Google, after all, is

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:33.822
in the private sector?

00:36:33.822 --> 00:36:34.780
MASSIMO MASCARO: It is.

00:36:34.780 --> 00:36:38.489
So FDL is a really, really
cool program that NASA runs.

00:36:38.489 --> 00:36:40.030
What they do is they
seek partnership

00:36:40.030 --> 00:36:43.000
with basically private
companies to help out

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.760
on some of the biggest
challenges that NASA has.

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:49.110
One of the big themes that FDL
research about-- in fact, I

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:51.262
would say is one of the
most prominent ones--

00:36:51.262 --> 00:36:52.720
is actually artificial
intelligence

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:54.760
applied to NASA's problem.

00:36:54.760 --> 00:36:55.970
So Google--

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:58.221
SETH SHOSTAK: Can you give
me an example of a problem?

00:36:58.221 --> 00:36:59.761
MASSIMO MASCARO:
For example, I mean,

00:36:59.761 --> 00:37:01.270
one of the things
actually that we

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:03.680
work on this year is
actually a big problem

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:05.290
in artificial
intelligence, is how

00:37:05.290 --> 00:37:07.300
do you actually
automatically detect

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:09.490
transiting of planets
from the signals

00:37:09.490 --> 00:37:11.440
that you get from
your space telescopes.

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:13.990
It is a problem where if
you want machine learning

00:37:13.990 --> 00:37:16.374
is very, very useful tool.

00:37:16.374 --> 00:37:18.040
Because right now
actually a lot of this

00:37:18.040 --> 00:37:20.950
is done manually, by
actual astronomers

00:37:20.950 --> 00:37:23.890
and there is actually
high error rates in that.

00:37:23.890 --> 00:37:26.110
SETH SHOSTAK: So
in the cloud you

00:37:26.110 --> 00:37:28.390
have, of course, the
opportunity for sharing data.

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:29.765
Anybody can get
to it presumably,

00:37:29.765 --> 00:37:31.723
if they're interested in
the problem of looking

00:37:31.723 --> 00:37:34.024
for planets around other
stars, just to follow up

00:37:34.024 --> 00:37:34.690
on your example.

00:37:34.690 --> 00:37:35.800
They can get to the data.

00:37:35.800 --> 00:37:37.675
But they can get to
something else there too.

00:37:37.675 --> 00:37:38.916
They can get to tools, right?

00:37:38.916 --> 00:37:39.790
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yes.

00:37:39.790 --> 00:37:42.651
So there are, I think,
actually three main things

00:37:42.651 --> 00:37:43.900
that you can get in the cloud.

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:45.691
So the first one, of
course, is the ability

00:37:45.691 --> 00:37:49.370
to get a lot of data in and have
very, very fast access to it.

00:37:49.370 --> 00:37:53.530
That's actually bread and butter
and you have to have that.

00:37:53.530 --> 00:37:54.910
The other thing is computation.

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:57.040
So on the cloud you can
get a lot of computation

00:37:57.040 --> 00:37:59.412
to run on top of that data.

00:37:59.412 --> 00:38:01.120
And that actually is
also very important.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:02.828
And this actually, by
the way, in science

00:38:02.828 --> 00:38:04.567
it's not like a super
easy thing to do.

00:38:04.567 --> 00:38:05.650
I mean, believe it or not.

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:07.399
I mean, some of the
largest supercomputers

00:38:07.399 --> 00:38:08.649
today are used for science.

00:38:08.649 --> 00:38:10.690
But actually, they're not
very available to a lot

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:11.650
of scientists.

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:14.080
And then the third
thing is in Cloud

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:17.170
we built a series of
higher level of things that

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:20.740
allow you to do things from
analytics and data processing

00:38:20.740 --> 00:38:23.470
to AI and machine learning.

00:38:23.470 --> 00:38:26.000
They actually are tools that
you build on top of these two

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:27.297
layers of data and computation.

00:38:27.297 --> 00:38:29.380
They allow you to do those
kind of things actually

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:30.305
much more easily.

00:38:30.305 --> 00:38:31.930
SETH SHOSTAK: Now,
suppose-- all right,

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:33.340
I'm a research scientist.

00:38:33.340 --> 00:38:35.350
I kind of know what
they look like.

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:37.019
There are a lot of
them near my office.

00:38:37.019 --> 00:38:38.310
They're sitting in their cubes.

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:39.780
They're sitting in their
offices-- whatever.

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:41.230
And they're on a desk
and in the corner

00:38:41.230 --> 00:38:43.870
there's a stack of reprints that
only five people in the world

00:38:43.870 --> 00:38:45.135
can actually understand.

00:38:45.135 --> 00:38:46.510
And they're working
on a problem.

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:48.659
Maybe that problem
is exactly this one.

00:38:48.659 --> 00:38:50.950
They want to know are there
a lot of planets out there?

00:38:50.950 --> 00:38:53.110
Are there not a lot
of planets out there?

00:38:53.110 --> 00:38:55.720
Now, in the past, they would
have to go get some data

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:57.130
themselves.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:59.980
And they would have exclusive
use of that data at least

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:01.930
for a while, even if
it was from the Hubble

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:05.620
telescope or some
publicly owned instrument.

00:39:05.620 --> 00:39:07.990
And now, they need to
go through those data

00:39:07.990 --> 00:39:10.450
and take a look is there
something interesting in it.

00:39:10.450 --> 00:39:12.130
They'd write some
code themselves.

00:39:12.130 --> 00:39:13.530
It would be proprietary.

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:15.280
They would probably
spend half their lives

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:17.260
debugging their own code.

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:18.980
And they would have
a limited data set

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:20.740
and that's how they would work.

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:22.720
Now, how is that
going to happen now?

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:25.054
I mean, I'm sitting in
my office wondering,

00:39:25.054 --> 00:39:27.512
hey, look, how am I going to
beat all those competitors out

00:39:27.512 --> 00:39:28.011
there?

00:39:28.011 --> 00:39:28.511
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:28.511 --> 00:39:30.469
MASSIMO MASCARO: So I
mean, a couple of things.

00:39:30.469 --> 00:39:31.899
I mean, of course,
scientific data

00:39:31.899 --> 00:39:33.440
very often actually
come from sources

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:34.439
like what you mentioned.

00:39:34.439 --> 00:39:38.090
So for example, I can tell you
that in the work that we've

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:40.326
been doing with
FDL on exoplanets,

00:39:40.326 --> 00:39:42.200
we've been working with
Kepler's data that is

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:43.580
now actually a public data set.

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:47.060
But there is a delay because
there are certain research

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:50.180
institutions that usually have
exclusiveness on those data.

00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:53.950
For example, we haven't worked
yet on the newly test data.

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:55.230
That is the new--

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:56.900
so for those who
don't know Kepler

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:59.145
is the old exoplanet
source satellite.

00:39:59.145 --> 00:40:00.020
SETH SHOSTAK: Oldish.

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:00.990
Dead for what, a month?

00:40:00.990 --> 00:40:01.490
[LAUGHS]

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:03.448
MASSIMO MASCARO: A month,
but has been actually

00:40:03.448 --> 00:40:05.780
in space for 10 years or more.

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:07.670
And TESS is just newly launched.

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:10.520
I mean, they launched it
with SpaceX last April.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:14.250
And TESS actually is in
orbit and is collecting data,

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:17.330
but that data is
not available yet.

00:40:17.330 --> 00:40:20.360
It is available to NASA and
to some of these research

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:22.692
scientists, but
not to the public.

00:40:22.692 --> 00:40:23.900
So two things actually there.

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:25.858
Of course, you have to
have access to the data.

00:40:25.858 --> 00:40:28.857
But very, very large data
sets are now becoming public.

00:40:28.857 --> 00:40:30.440
And in any case, it
is always possible

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:33.740
even if you have private
data sets to work with Google

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:37.890
and have it imported in a
private setup inside the cloud.

00:40:37.890 --> 00:40:39.890
The cloud doesn't have
to be public in that it's

00:40:39.890 --> 00:40:41.514
open to everyone, if
you don't want to.

00:40:41.514 --> 00:40:44.360
We have a lot of like, if you
want, commercial customers, who

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:47.730
have very, very sensitive
data that do not share.

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:48.590
So that's possible.

00:40:48.590 --> 00:40:50.610
But I would say that the
very exciting thing is

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:52.400
when you work on
these public data sets

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:54.430
and now actually you have
access to these data.

00:40:54.430 --> 00:40:56.180
But very often having
access to the data--

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:58.880
I can download all the Kepler
data sets from NASA today,

00:40:58.880 --> 00:40:59.791
if I want to.

00:40:59.791 --> 00:41:01.790
But the point is, what
am I going to do with it?

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:03.680
Because first of
all, it's likely not

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:05.890
going to fit inside my computer.

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:08.750
And even if it does, then
actually processing that data

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:11.450
requires very extensive
computational capabilities

00:41:11.450 --> 00:41:12.560
that I wouldn't have.

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:16.160
And the Cloud allow you to have
those very, very cheap price.

00:41:16.160 --> 00:41:17.870
Now, in the work
actually with NASA,

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:20.280
Google sponsored that,
so that was not an issue.

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:25.960
But in general, even if
somebody had to pay it,

00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:27.830
you only pay for what
you use actually.

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:30.165
Google actually will charge
you by the second literally.

00:41:30.165 --> 00:41:31.790
When you have a
machine, if you have it

00:41:31.790 --> 00:41:35.099
for 1 minute and 37 seconds to
do a particular computation,

00:41:35.099 --> 00:41:36.890
that's exactly what
you'll get charged for.

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:39.271
And so this actually is very
transformative, I think,

00:41:39.271 --> 00:41:39.770
in science.

00:41:39.770 --> 00:41:41.750
Because now I have
these very large data

00:41:41.750 --> 00:41:44.480
sets that are becoming
more and more available,

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:49.990
I have the ability to spawn
with a very cheap price, very

00:41:49.990 --> 00:41:51.950
large computation to
analyze that data sets,

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:53.540
and I have this whole
layer of tooling

00:41:53.540 --> 00:41:56.123
that, for example, allow me to
create a machine learning model

00:41:56.123 --> 00:41:59.450
or to run a very sophisticated
analysis that before actually

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.840
would have required maybe me to
get extra time slot on a very

00:42:02.840 --> 00:42:06.170
big supercomputer that maybe my
department doesn't have access

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:06.710
to.

00:42:06.710 --> 00:42:10.040
So this really allows
everyone to do this.

00:42:10.040 --> 00:42:13.639
And I would actually
put it even beyond that.

00:42:13.639 --> 00:42:15.680
There are actually a lot
of initiatives right now

00:42:15.680 --> 00:42:18.290
around citizenship science--

00:42:18.290 --> 00:42:20.660
people that are not
officially scientists

00:42:20.660 --> 00:42:22.700
starting to do real science.

00:42:22.700 --> 00:42:24.380
And I think, actually
this opens up

00:42:24.380 --> 00:42:26.820
a lot of floodgates of
that to happen as well.

00:42:26.820 --> 00:42:29.403
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, I was going
to say this sounds like taking

00:42:29.403 --> 00:42:31.140
citizen science to the max.

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:33.950
I mean, right, because now
everybody can get to the data.

00:42:33.950 --> 00:42:37.280
But beyond that, everybody
has some very competently

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:39.077
written, presumably,
tools to allow

00:42:39.077 --> 00:42:40.160
them to look at that data.

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:41.576
So they can look
for correlations.

00:42:41.576 --> 00:42:44.130
Or presumably,
they could say, OK,

00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:45.860
what I want to find
is some sort of dip

00:42:45.860 --> 00:42:47.657
in the brightness of
stars that tells me

00:42:47.657 --> 00:42:50.240
that that star has some planets
going around-- whatever it is.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:52.140
Can you give me another
example of the kinds of things

00:42:52.140 --> 00:42:52.340
that you could use this for?

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:54.798
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah, and this
actually is another example

00:42:54.798 --> 00:42:57.410
that we worked on this summer
that is also opening up

00:42:57.410 --> 00:42:59.790
another stream of thought.

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:01.370
In the second work
we did with NASA,

00:43:01.370 --> 00:43:03.290
we were working on astrobiology.

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:06.457
And one of the big problems
in astrobiology is,

00:43:06.457 --> 00:43:08.040
assuming there are
planets out there--

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:10.250
and now we know actually that
there are many exoplanets out

00:43:10.250 --> 00:43:11.781
there outside of
our solar system--

00:43:11.781 --> 00:43:14.030
we want to know actually
whether there is life or not.

00:43:14.030 --> 00:43:16.250
The problem of knowing
whether there's life or not

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:18.740
is to also understand
what life is.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:21.200
I mean, there are many different
kinds of possible lives

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:23.717
that would be conceivable.

00:43:23.717 --> 00:43:25.800
And in a sense, actually
we only have one sample--

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:27.740
it's what is on Earth.

00:43:27.740 --> 00:43:30.950
So one way around this is
that you could simulate

00:43:30.950 --> 00:43:32.460
what life could look like.

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:34.680
And there are
actually various ways.

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:38.450
The thing actually that we
did with FDL this summer

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:41.080
was to basically simulate
metabolic pathways.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:44.510
So different combinations
of molecules that bond

00:43:44.510 --> 00:43:45.010
chemically--

00:43:45.010 --> 00:43:45.980
SETH SHOSTAK: Well,
I've got to follow up

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:47.480
on this, because
you can simulate

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:49.730
what extraterrestrial
life might be like.

00:43:49.730 --> 00:43:53.642
Now, look, I get called up every
third week by some producer

00:43:53.642 --> 00:43:55.100
down in Hollywood
and they're going

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:57.641
to make a film about how aliens
have come to Earth to destroy

00:43:57.641 --> 00:44:00.059
downtown Chicago or whatever--
whatever is on their minds.

00:44:00.059 --> 00:44:02.600
And they want to know what are
the aliens going to look like.

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:03.350
Now, it sounds like--

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:03.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:03.860 --> 00:44:05.240
--you've got the tools
to figure that out.

00:44:05.240 --> 00:44:07.220
I mean, surely that's not
exactly what you mean.

00:44:07.220 --> 00:44:07.870
MASSIMO MASCARO: No.

00:44:07.870 --> 00:44:09.600
It is much less
glamorous, if you want.

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:10.975
But actually in
a sense, actually

00:44:10.975 --> 00:44:12.320
is also much more interesting.

00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:15.590
A lot of what we're
thinking about life today

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:20.150
in terms of humanoid figures and
so on, I mean we can actually

00:44:20.150 --> 00:44:21.970
go there but it's likely--

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:24.140
it's actually going
to be very unlikely.

00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:28.730
The vast majority of life that
we expect to find on exoplanets

00:44:28.730 --> 00:44:31.840
may be just unicellular life.

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:33.530
Or it may not even
cellular-- may just

00:44:33.530 --> 00:44:38.270
be some kind of chemical
processes that go on that

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:41.120
signal the fact that there
is actually something

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:42.740
that can be self-reproducing.

00:44:42.740 --> 00:44:44.720
SETH SHOSTAK: So in
other words, to begin

00:44:44.720 --> 00:44:46.730
with the whole question
of what life is--

00:44:46.730 --> 00:44:48.230
I mean, that's a
different question.

00:44:48.230 --> 00:44:50.000
And there's actually
no good answer to that.

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:51.710
I many people say,
well, life reproduces,

00:44:51.710 --> 00:44:56.300
and metabolic processes
and all that stuff,

00:44:56.300 --> 00:44:58.254
uses resources,
maybe moves around.

00:44:58.254 --> 00:45:00.170
But that's true of a lot
of physical phenomena

00:45:00.170 --> 00:45:02.378
that are not alive, like
fire or something like that.

00:45:02.378 --> 00:45:05.210
So in fact, it's not that
you're trying to define life.

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:08.480
You're trying to say what
chemical processes might

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:12.330
lead to something that has
the characteristics of life.

00:45:12.330 --> 00:45:12.830
Right?

00:45:12.830 --> 00:45:13.435
Is that it?

00:45:13.435 --> 00:45:14.393
MASSIMO MASCARO: It is.

00:45:14.393 --> 00:45:18.480
And in a sense, it actually
is a restrictive idea of life,

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:23.600
because there may be many, many
larger ways of intending it.

00:45:23.600 --> 00:45:25.670
Life could be
completely digital or it

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:28.560
could be actually not even
based on necessary chemistry.

00:45:28.560 --> 00:45:31.599
But in the realm of life
that is based on chemistry,

00:45:31.599 --> 00:45:33.890
one thing that we know at
least on Earth that life does

00:45:33.890 --> 00:45:37.657
is that it changes the chemical
compositions of planets.

00:45:37.657 --> 00:45:39.740
In particular, it changes
the chemical composition

00:45:39.740 --> 00:45:41.114
of atmospheres of planets.

00:45:41.114 --> 00:45:42.530
And the reason
this is interesting

00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:46.134
is that we have a shot at
measuring the atmospheres

00:45:46.134 --> 00:45:47.300
of some of these exoplanets.

00:45:47.300 --> 00:45:50.136
Now, to be clear, today
we cannot do that yet.

00:45:50.136 --> 00:45:52.010
At least that's what my
NASA friends tell me.

00:45:52.010 --> 00:45:55.739
[LAUGHS] But in
the near future--

00:45:55.739 --> 00:45:57.530
I mean, apparently the
James Webb telescope

00:45:57.530 --> 00:45:59.120
may be able to do this.

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:02.199
We may be able to detect spectra
of some of these exoplanets.

00:46:02.199 --> 00:46:04.490
SETH SHOSTAK: Just to clarify
for people who don't work

00:46:04.490 --> 00:46:06.980
in this field, if you
were to look at Earth,

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:09.010
if you were a
cling-on astronomer--

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:09.674
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:09.674 --> 00:46:11.840
--I hope they get paid
better than the earthly kind.

00:46:11.840 --> 00:46:13.340
But if you were a
cling-on astronomy

00:46:13.340 --> 00:46:15.740
and you had a big enough
telescope like James Webb

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:17.690
and you looked at the
Earth with that thing,

00:46:17.690 --> 00:46:19.670
you might be able
to find that there's

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:21.350
oxygen in the atmosphere.

00:46:21.350 --> 00:46:23.000
20% of the air in this room--

00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:25.680
oxygen. But that oxygen
comes from photosynthesis,

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:28.090
so they would say, oh, I
don't know what's there,

00:46:28.090 --> 00:46:32.780
Zork, but all that oxygen there
must be lettuce or asparagus

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:35.570
or something that
does photosynthesis.

00:46:35.570 --> 00:46:37.430
But that might not be
the only indicator.

00:46:37.430 --> 00:46:40.130
Maybe they don't
breathe out oxygen

00:46:40.130 --> 00:46:42.740
if they're plants
on another planet.

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:43.480
Is that the idea?

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:44.110
MASSIMO MASCARO:
That's the idea.

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:45.485
And the one thing
actually I want

00:46:45.485 --> 00:46:47.300
to say for people who
are not in this field

00:46:47.300 --> 00:46:49.280
is that you could actually
say, well, I mean,

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:51.560
couldn't oxygen just
kind of be on that planet

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:52.460
for other reasons?

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:55.760
Well, we know that oxygen binds
a lot to a lot of chemicals.

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:58.670
So it's very likely
that oxygen stays

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:00.320
in an atmosphere
for a long period,

00:47:00.320 --> 00:47:02.240
because it would
just oxidize most

00:47:02.240 --> 00:47:04.580
of the stuff that is in that
planet and basically bind--

00:47:04.580 --> 00:47:06.090
so basically, disappear
from the atmosphere.

00:47:06.090 --> 00:47:07.250
SETH SHOSTAK: And it
would turn all the iron

00:47:07.250 --> 00:47:08.489
in the soil to rust.

00:47:08.489 --> 00:47:09.780
Like Mars, it would become red.

00:47:09.780 --> 00:47:10.120
MASSIMO MASCARO: Pretty much.

00:47:10.120 --> 00:47:11.720
Mars is a good example.

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:14.300
So the fact that there is
oxygen is because something

00:47:14.300 --> 00:47:15.950
is actively producing it.

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:20.510
Now, there are various ways in
which oxygen could be actively

00:47:20.510 --> 00:47:23.532
produced by no life systems.

00:47:23.532 --> 00:47:25.490
But we know actually of
a lot of metabolic ways

00:47:25.490 --> 00:47:26.790
in which this happens.

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:27.890
And actually, oxygen,
to your point,

00:47:27.890 --> 00:47:29.015
is not just the only thing.

00:47:29.015 --> 00:47:31.520
I mean, in fact, one of the
things actually they told me--

00:47:31.520 --> 00:47:33.950
the astrobiologists
that we work this year--

00:47:33.950 --> 00:47:35.420
is that actually
in a sense oxygen

00:47:35.420 --> 00:47:37.520
is almost like the
smoking gun of life.

00:47:37.520 --> 00:47:41.270
Because an oxygen level like
the one that we have on Earth

00:47:41.270 --> 00:47:43.100
is almost unlikely to
be actually just kind

00:47:43.100 --> 00:47:45.392
of due to geological sources.

00:47:45.392 --> 00:47:46.850
But there are many
other chemicals.

00:47:46.850 --> 00:47:48.560
For example, just
looking at the ratio

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:52.860
of methane and oxygen or
methane and other components--

00:47:52.860 --> 00:47:53.744
sulfur.

00:47:53.744 --> 00:47:55.160
There are a lot
of other chemicals

00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:59.300
that could actually
manifest signatures of life

00:47:59.300 --> 00:48:00.280
being on those planets.

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:03.560
So going back to cloud
and what the problem is,

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:06.650
is that very often there's
digital signatures of what

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:09.530
would be the relative
components of these chemicals

00:48:09.530 --> 00:48:11.210
in atmospheres.

00:48:11.210 --> 00:48:12.890
We only know them for Earth.

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:15.140
And if you want to know
what the signature will

00:48:15.140 --> 00:48:20.570
look like for different
reasonable metabolic pathways,

00:48:20.570 --> 00:48:22.310
you would have to simulate that.

00:48:22.310 --> 00:48:24.980
And this actually is
what we did with Google.

00:48:24.980 --> 00:48:26.690
And one thing, again,
I mean, going back

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:29.930
to the initial
discussion, one thing that

00:48:29.930 --> 00:48:33.410
was very useful is the fact
that these simulations are

00:48:33.410 --> 00:48:36.800
very expensive computationally.

00:48:36.800 --> 00:48:38.330
And in a sense,
actually, it would

00:48:38.330 --> 00:48:41.900
take years to run those things
on just even very, very fancy

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:44.390
hardware that a
normal university has.

00:48:44.390 --> 00:48:46.490
We were able to spin up
on Google Cloud thousands

00:48:46.490 --> 00:48:48.380
of machines in parallel.

00:48:48.380 --> 00:48:51.590
And basically the bulk of the
data set that was produced

00:48:51.590 --> 00:48:54.040
was produced in a matter
of a couple of weeks.

00:48:54.040 --> 00:48:56.150
But just actually spawning
it to a lot of machines

00:48:56.150 --> 00:48:56.930
inside our data centers.

00:48:56.930 --> 00:48:58.430
SETH SHOSTAK: I got
to say, Massimo,

00:48:58.430 --> 00:49:01.640
because I know somebody--
well, Sarah Seager is her name.

00:49:01.640 --> 00:49:03.710
She's at MIT and
this is her job.

00:49:03.710 --> 00:49:07.290
It's to try and figure out, OK,
if we can measure the whatever

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:11.009
is in the atmosphere or some
planet around some other star,

00:49:11.009 --> 00:49:12.425
can we say that
there's life there

00:49:12.425 --> 00:49:14.210
or there's not life there.

00:49:14.210 --> 00:49:16.280
Are people like that
using the Google Cloud?

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:17.750
Are they already
using these tools

00:49:17.750 --> 00:49:19.876
or is this coming down the
pike in the near future?

00:49:19.876 --> 00:49:22.250
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah, I think,
actually it's picking up.

00:49:22.250 --> 00:49:24.830
So I know that there are a
number of research institutes

00:49:24.830 --> 00:49:30.320
and scientific institutions
that are really using Google

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:32.240
Cloud and cloud in general.

00:49:32.240 --> 00:49:35.210
I think, one of the things
actually we did at NASA today

00:49:35.210 --> 00:49:37.760
this summer and
that in a sense I'm

00:49:37.760 --> 00:49:40.220
really hopeful it
creates some movement,

00:49:40.220 --> 00:49:42.930
is the fact that we proved
how this can be useful.

00:49:42.930 --> 00:49:47.480
Now, one other aspect of
NASA's FDL program to get clear

00:49:47.480 --> 00:49:50.300
is that it's a program
that lasts about 10 weeks.

00:49:50.300 --> 00:49:52.340
So basically, there
are the scientists

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:55.580
to come and work at actually
FDL here in Mountain View.

00:49:55.580 --> 00:49:59.252
And in 10 weeks, they just
have to start and end.

00:49:59.252 --> 00:50:01.460
They literally have to finish
their technical reports

00:50:01.460 --> 00:50:02.610
in 10 weeks.

00:50:02.610 --> 00:50:04.670
And so one other thing
I want to throw in there

00:50:04.670 --> 00:50:06.620
is that it doesn't take much.

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:08.090
These guys literally
arrived, they

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:11.634
had absolutely no knowledge
of Google Cloud at all.

00:50:11.634 --> 00:50:13.550
I remember actually we
had a meeting at Google

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:15.920
and we were busy explaining
how to spin up machines,

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:19.255
how to train machine learning
models, and there were--

00:50:19.255 --> 00:50:20.630
I mean, most of
them actually had

00:50:20.630 --> 00:50:21.921
absolutely no prior experience.

00:50:21.921 --> 00:50:23.750
And they went
actually in 10 weeks

00:50:23.750 --> 00:50:26.285
to publish some very
interesting results.

00:50:26.285 --> 00:50:28.285
And now actually they're
talking at conferences.

00:50:28.285 --> 00:50:29.702
They're publishing
papers on this.

00:50:29.702 --> 00:50:31.326
SETH SHOSTAK: All
right, so what you're

00:50:31.326 --> 00:50:33.170
saying is scientists
who are already

00:50:33.170 --> 00:50:35.234
doing this or scientists
who want to do this,

00:50:35.234 --> 00:50:36.650
they're not going
to have to spend

00:50:36.650 --> 00:50:38.281
years learning how to do it.

00:50:38.281 --> 00:50:38.780
Right?

00:50:38.780 --> 00:50:38.840
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yes.

00:50:38.840 --> 00:50:39.920
SETH SHOSTAK: I mean,
they can get trained up

00:50:39.920 --> 00:50:41.570
in a couple of
weeks and they can

00:50:41.570 --> 00:50:42.900
start doing something useful.

00:50:42.900 --> 00:50:43.560
MASSIMO MASCARO: I think so.

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:45.476
And I think, actually,
this is transformative.

00:50:45.476 --> 00:50:48.720
Because at the end of the
day, a lot of modern science

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:51.680
is not anymore
just genius people

00:50:51.680 --> 00:50:55.300
sitting down with paper and pen
and having a brilliant idea.

00:50:55.300 --> 00:50:57.980
It's a lot actually has
to do with data analysis,

00:50:57.980 --> 00:50:59.845
with computation,
with simulation.

00:50:59.845 --> 00:51:01.970
And I think, actually,
cloud can be transformative.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:03.830
Now, this actually still
we haven't touched yet

00:51:03.830 --> 00:51:05.788
even into the realm of
machine learning and AI.

00:51:05.788 --> 00:51:08.450
But I think it's also very,
very interesting and exciting.

00:51:08.450 --> 00:51:11.740
But one thing is just actually
the basic of science, I think,

00:51:11.740 --> 00:51:13.490
actually cloud can be
very transformative.

00:51:13.490 --> 00:51:16.910
And I think it is just a
matter of sometimes of culture

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:18.620
and being used to.

00:51:18.620 --> 00:51:21.240
A lot of researchers today
do not know yet these tools.

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:22.970
They haven't experienced them.

00:51:22.970 --> 00:51:24.860
And in a sense,
actually, I'm pretty

00:51:24.860 --> 00:51:28.100
sure that as soon as they
get to know them better,

00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:30.740
they'll find actually that they
can change a lot how they work.

00:51:30.740 --> 00:51:33.073
SETH SHOSTAK: Let me take
another specific example here.

00:51:33.073 --> 00:51:35.990
In the 1970s, NASA sent
a couple of landers--

00:51:35.990 --> 00:51:39.140
the Viking landers-- to
Mars, looking for life

00:51:39.140 --> 00:51:40.740
on our little ruddy
buddy up there.

00:51:40.740 --> 00:51:41.560
Right?

00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:43.060
And the answer they
came back with--

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:44.180
I mean, this cost
a lot of money.

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:45.530
It was like a billion
dollars or something.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:46.850
I don't remember exactly.

00:51:46.850 --> 00:51:50.140
But the answer that came
back was don't know.

00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:52.140
We don't think we
found any life,

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:54.155
but of course, we
can't be 100% positive.

00:51:54.155 --> 00:51:55.880
Well, obviously,
the space agency

00:51:55.880 --> 00:51:57.620
wasn't really happy
with this answer.

00:51:57.620 --> 00:51:59.346
And I suppose the
taxpayers to the extent

00:51:59.346 --> 00:52:01.970
that they paid attention weren't
very happy either, because you

00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:03.553
spent all this money,
you want to know

00:52:03.553 --> 00:52:05.707
if there's life on Mars,
and it comes back maybe.

00:52:05.707 --> 00:52:06.540
You don't want that.

00:52:06.540 --> 00:52:08.623
You want a definitive
answer like 42 or something.

00:52:08.623 --> 00:52:10.430
You want a definitive answer.

00:52:10.430 --> 00:52:14.480
Now, if they had had the
Google Cloud and these tools

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:16.730
back in the mid
1970s, do you think

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:19.160
they would have had a better
answer in the sense of a more

00:52:19.160 --> 00:52:21.574
definitive answer?

00:52:21.574 --> 00:52:23.990
MASSIMO MASCARO: I don't know
because that's actually also

00:52:23.990 --> 00:52:26.060
whether the answer was
in the data or not.

00:52:26.060 --> 00:52:28.921
So what the cloud cannot give
you is answers that are not

00:52:28.921 --> 00:52:29.420
in the data.

00:52:29.420 --> 00:52:34.490
That said, I think that
for a long time in science

00:52:34.490 --> 00:52:37.910
we've actually experienced
this idea that people

00:52:37.910 --> 00:52:39.890
work on a particular
problem, they

00:52:39.890 --> 00:52:43.190
do an experiment-- let's say
you send an actual NASA probe

00:52:43.190 --> 00:52:46.970
or sometimes actually you
do an experiment in a lab.

00:52:46.970 --> 00:52:48.620
You collect a lot of data.

00:52:48.620 --> 00:52:51.650
You process them in the
best thing which you can.

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:54.200
And actually, you
publish a paper-- maybe?

00:52:54.200 --> 00:52:55.290
And that's it.

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:56.769
That's the end of that data.

00:52:56.769 --> 00:52:59.060
I think, actually the idea
that this data sets actually

00:52:59.060 --> 00:53:00.060
now become public.

00:53:00.060 --> 00:53:01.640
And these data sets
are becoming big.

00:53:01.640 --> 00:53:05.270
And that people now from all
over the scientific and new

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:07.961
scientific community can go
there and look at those data.

00:53:07.961 --> 00:53:10.460
It means that you can now start
squeezing actually much more

00:53:10.460 --> 00:53:12.800
information out of
the same data sets.

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:14.660
And actually we can
both validate better

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:16.110
our research result.

00:53:16.110 --> 00:53:18.452
And we can also, if we want
to find things that actually

00:53:18.452 --> 00:53:19.410
had gone undiscovered--

00:53:19.410 --> 00:53:22.490
I mean, case in
point, Kepler data.

00:53:22.490 --> 00:53:24.890
Kepler has been
there for 10 years.

00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:28.082
And with the help of Kepler,
NASA has been able to declare--

00:53:28.082 --> 00:53:29.540
I don't remember
the number-- but I

00:53:29.540 --> 00:53:32.531
think, actually it's
north of 2,500 exoplanets,

00:53:32.531 --> 00:53:33.530
if I remember correctly.

00:53:33.530 --> 00:53:34.870
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, a
little more actually.

00:53:34.870 --> 00:53:36.110
MASSIMO MASCARO:
Yeah, a little more.

00:53:36.110 --> 00:53:36.860
SETH SHOSTAK: A lot.

00:53:36.860 --> 00:53:38.390
MASSIMO MASCARO: Last
year, actually, Google

00:53:38.390 --> 00:53:39.400
took that data set.

00:53:39.400 --> 00:53:41.140
And this is not
part of this FDL,

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:44.330
this was Google Brain, our
AI Research Department.

00:53:44.330 --> 00:53:46.340
Took the data set
and it basically

00:53:46.340 --> 00:53:47.930
applied machine learning to it.

00:53:47.930 --> 00:53:49.790
And it did discover a
number of exoplanets

00:53:49.790 --> 00:53:50.920
that had gone undiscovered.

00:53:50.920 --> 00:53:53.240
In fact, they discovered
an entire exoplanet system

00:53:53.240 --> 00:53:55.760
that looks quite like the
Earth, that have completely

00:53:55.760 --> 00:53:56.510
gone undiscovered.

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:59.600
Now, I mean, on one
side is the ability

00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:01.109
to have access to the data.

00:54:01.109 --> 00:54:02.150
So that's very important.

00:54:02.150 --> 00:54:04.275
And I think, actually NASA's
doing an excellent job

00:54:04.275 --> 00:54:05.840
in making all those
data sets public.

00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:08.290
And I think a lot of
other institutions

00:54:08.290 --> 00:54:11.580
and even university labs
should just follow suit.

00:54:11.580 --> 00:54:13.670
And some are, but even more.

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:15.990
Every data set in my
opinion in science,

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:18.740
especially if it's publicly
funded, should be public.

00:54:18.740 --> 00:54:20.360
But then that's not
sufficient either.

00:54:20.360 --> 00:54:22.541
Because now I have
this gigantic data set,

00:54:22.541 --> 00:54:24.290
and there is no way
if I don't have access

00:54:24.290 --> 00:54:26.330
to the NASA supercomputer,
I can actually

00:54:26.330 --> 00:54:28.050
extract information from it.

00:54:28.050 --> 00:54:32.200
So the idea that now actually as
somebody-- the guys at Google,

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:32.980
actually--

00:54:32.980 --> 00:54:35.140
it was done actually
with an astronomer

00:54:35.140 --> 00:54:37.720
at the University of Texas,
if I remember correctly.

00:54:37.720 --> 00:54:39.430
But the other guy
actually here at Google

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:41.830
was just an AI engineer.

00:54:41.830 --> 00:54:43.930
And they basically sat
down together and said,

00:54:43.930 --> 00:54:45.430
OK, let's reanalyze this.

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:47.210
And they found exoplanet
that got missing.

00:54:47.210 --> 00:54:50.230
So I think, actually, this
is a great opportunity for us

00:54:50.230 --> 00:54:52.810
to extract more information
out of the data sets that

00:54:52.810 --> 00:54:54.940
very often costly we collect.

00:54:54.940 --> 00:54:58.026
And actually, to also understand
better what's sometimes

00:54:58.026 --> 00:54:59.650
actually wrong,
because maybe sometimes

00:54:59.650 --> 00:55:01.899
actually the problem is that
an analysis that has been

00:55:01.899 --> 00:55:03.280
done actually may be wrong.

00:55:03.280 --> 00:55:05.916
And the idea that other
people can look at the data

00:55:05.916 --> 00:55:07.540
actually makes it
much more verifiable.

00:55:07.540 --> 00:55:09.980
That is actually one of
the principle of science.

00:55:09.980 --> 00:55:11.813
SETH SHOSTAK: There are
coming down the pike

00:55:11.813 --> 00:55:13.346
some really big telescopes.

00:55:13.346 --> 00:55:15.220
And we're talking about
astronomy here a lot.

00:55:15.220 --> 00:55:16.902
But they're--

00:55:16.902 --> 00:55:18.110
MASSIMO MASCARO: That's nice.

00:55:18.110 --> 00:55:18.850
SETH SHOSTAK:
Well, that's right.

00:55:18.850 --> 00:55:20.308
There's going to
be telescopes that

00:55:20.308 --> 00:55:22.210
can look at the entire
sky, every four days,

00:55:22.210 --> 00:55:23.164
that sort of thing.

00:55:23.164 --> 00:55:24.830
See things that we've
never seen before.

00:55:24.830 --> 00:55:27.730
I mean, there could be
flashing lights in the sky,

00:55:27.730 --> 00:55:30.070
we would never see them because
the telescopes are never

00:55:30.070 --> 00:55:31.945
looking in the right
place at the right time.

00:55:31.945 --> 00:55:34.111
So we're going to have
instruments that can do that.

00:55:34.111 --> 00:55:36.310
We're also building these
huge telescopes here

00:55:36.310 --> 00:55:38.586
with mirrors that are
20, 30 meters across.

00:55:38.586 --> 00:55:40.960
And obviously, they're going
to be able to do things that

00:55:40.960 --> 00:55:42.460
our current telescopes can't.

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:45.320
So the instrumentation is
certainly getting a lot better.

00:55:45.320 --> 00:55:49.480
But it produces enormous
tons, mountains of data

00:55:49.480 --> 00:55:51.080
that nobody can go
through anymore.

00:55:51.080 --> 00:55:52.720
This is not like
in the old days--

00:55:52.720 --> 00:55:54.970
when I was a kid just
before the Crimean War.

00:55:54.970 --> 00:55:57.832
When I was a kid you
would go to the telescope,

00:55:57.832 --> 00:56:00.040
you'd put a photographic
plate at the back end of it,

00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:01.414
you'd make a
photograph, and then

00:56:01.414 --> 00:56:04.094
that plate would go
in your desk drawer.

00:56:04.094 --> 00:56:05.260
Nobody else could get to it.

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:06.468
Nobody else could look at it.

00:56:06.468 --> 00:56:08.770
And maybe you didn't
ever look at it.

00:56:08.770 --> 00:56:10.990
So obviously, that's
going to change.

00:56:10.990 --> 00:56:12.670
It sounds like the
researchers are going

00:56:12.670 --> 00:56:14.509
to become data scientists.

00:56:14.509 --> 00:56:16.550
MASSIMO MASCARO: I think,
actually more and more.

00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:18.674
I mean, I think, actually
in part they already are.

00:56:18.674 --> 00:56:21.970
If you think about how most
of the research I have done

00:56:21.970 --> 00:56:25.660
personally was really at the
border between data science,

00:56:25.660 --> 00:56:26.410
data analysis.

00:56:26.410 --> 00:56:28.330
And actually, in
my case, actually I

00:56:28.330 --> 00:56:30.040
was studying computation
neuroscience.

00:56:30.040 --> 00:56:31.998
I was trying to understand
how the brain works.

00:56:31.998 --> 00:56:34.730
But it was a lot of
heavy data analysis.

00:56:34.730 --> 00:56:39.160
But the other aspect is that
these data sets, to your point,

00:56:39.160 --> 00:56:44.030
are becoming more
and more gigantic.

00:56:44.030 --> 00:56:47.230
And they actually are
more and more complex too.

00:56:47.230 --> 00:56:49.389
I mean, of these
telescopes require

00:56:49.389 --> 00:56:51.430
you to do all sorts of
processing into this data.

00:56:51.430 --> 00:56:52.804
And this processing
may sometimes

00:56:52.804 --> 00:56:53.899
actually change the data.

00:56:53.899 --> 00:56:55.690
So sometimes you have
to do different kinds

00:56:55.690 --> 00:56:56.440
of pre-processing.

00:56:56.440 --> 00:56:59.305
This actually was the case
of Kepler, for example.

00:56:59.305 --> 00:57:02.380
And so the idea that you have
now this vast computation

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:04.610
infrastructure, this vast
analytic infrastructure,

00:57:04.610 --> 00:57:06.370
and then actually we can talk
a little bit about machine

00:57:06.370 --> 00:57:07.520
learning as well,
because I think,

00:57:07.520 --> 00:57:09.850
actually, that's also going
to play a huge role there.

00:57:09.850 --> 00:57:13.120
I think, actually, that's
going to be transformative.

00:57:13.120 --> 00:57:15.670
Now, one last thing
actually on this point

00:57:15.670 --> 00:57:20.600
is the fact that I
think more and more

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:24.190
we're also starting to see data
collection happening outside

00:57:24.190 --> 00:57:29.680
of the realm of
gigantic instruments

00:57:29.680 --> 00:57:32.267
that, let's say, NASA
actually deploys.

00:57:32.267 --> 00:57:33.850
There is an interesting
project that I

00:57:33.850 --> 00:57:36.832
got to know about
where they put actually

00:57:36.832 --> 00:57:38.040
this kind of small telescope.

00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:40.780
These are almost amateurish
telescopes, basically

00:57:40.780 --> 00:57:43.270
all around the globe to
track these exoplanets.

00:57:43.270 --> 00:57:45.280
And the idea is
that TESS actually

00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:47.700
will only look at a certain
patch of the sky for 28

00:57:47.700 --> 00:57:49.356
days at a time.

00:57:49.356 --> 00:57:50.980
And so maybe actually
another telescope

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:53.284
will pick up from there
and start doing this.

00:57:53.284 --> 00:57:54.700
And again, this
actually organizes

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:56.770
data sets and becomes
really fundamental now

00:57:56.770 --> 00:57:58.700
to be able to go
across data sets.

00:57:58.700 --> 00:58:03.100
Not just being able to apply
the big guns of supercomputers

00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:04.885
or the cloud into
a particular data

00:58:04.885 --> 00:58:07.390
sets, but being able to
spawn between data sets that

00:58:07.390 --> 00:58:09.450
very often have very
different shapes, forms,

00:58:09.450 --> 00:58:11.200
and they need actually
very different kind

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:14.650
of pre-processing
and ability to part.

00:58:14.650 --> 00:58:17.069
So that actually also is
going across data sets.

00:58:17.069 --> 00:58:18.610
It's going to be
very transformative.

00:58:18.610 --> 00:58:20.026
SETH SHOSTAK: Well,
I have to say,

00:58:20.026 --> 00:58:21.520
I mean, in my line
of work, you're

00:58:21.520 --> 00:58:25.680
looking for a radio signal or
a light signal coming from ET.

00:58:25.680 --> 00:58:27.146
And we get lots of signals.

00:58:27.146 --> 00:58:28.520
It's not like in
the movies where

00:58:28.520 --> 00:58:30.130
you're sitting
around looking bored

00:58:30.130 --> 00:58:33.310
and suddenly you see a spike on
the screen and everybody jumps

00:58:33.310 --> 00:58:35.380
up and starts screaming
and calling up

00:58:35.380 --> 00:58:36.670
the press and all that.

00:58:36.670 --> 00:58:39.640
What actually happens is every
10 seconds you get a signal.

00:58:39.640 --> 00:58:43.480
But those signals can
be very messy looking.

00:58:43.480 --> 00:58:48.010
And AI might be just the ticket
to sort through those millions

00:58:48.010 --> 00:58:50.560
and millions of signals you're
getting every day and decide,

00:58:50.560 --> 00:58:53.650
OK, this is interference from
a satellite, this is maybe ET,

00:58:53.650 --> 00:58:55.520
this is not ET,
that kind of thing.

00:58:55.520 --> 00:58:59.460
I mean, personally, this sounds
like a very attractive thing.

00:58:59.460 --> 00:59:01.960
We've been talking a lot about
astronomy and space research.

00:59:01.960 --> 00:59:04.030
Can you give me another example?

00:59:04.030 --> 00:59:06.660
I'm just thinking there must
be huge data sets out there,

00:59:06.660 --> 00:59:09.580
which talk about
various diseases--

00:59:09.580 --> 00:59:11.420
people get a certain
kind of cancer.

00:59:11.420 --> 00:59:14.350
And this is maybe their diets
or their lifestyle or something

00:59:14.350 --> 00:59:17.110
else and nobody has
the ability to see

00:59:17.110 --> 00:59:19.720
if there's a slight
correlation with maybe eating

00:59:19.720 --> 00:59:21.942
broccoli and getting
cancer of the elbows

00:59:21.942 --> 00:59:22.900
or something like that.

00:59:22.900 --> 00:59:23.932
I mean--

00:59:23.932 --> 00:59:25.390
MASSIMO MASCARO:
Those actually are

00:59:25.390 --> 00:59:27.520
big topics you may have heard.

00:59:27.520 --> 00:59:30.310
Google actually is
very vested into health

00:59:30.310 --> 00:59:32.997
and we're working a lot in it.

00:59:32.997 --> 00:59:35.080
Actually very recently we
created a whole division

00:59:35.080 --> 00:59:38.290
called Google Health that
will work on those problems.

00:59:38.290 --> 00:59:40.630
Some of the early
results in that area

00:59:40.630 --> 00:59:44.558
are mind-boggling in that
it's not only about the fact

00:59:44.558 --> 00:59:47.057
that you can now actually infer
across very, very large data

00:59:47.057 --> 00:59:49.660
sets and understand
this is better.

00:59:49.660 --> 00:59:51.110
It's the fact that
very often you

00:59:51.110 --> 00:59:53.330
then actually get
also diagnosis that

00:59:53.330 --> 00:59:54.820
are much more democratized.

00:59:54.820 --> 00:59:57.490
So to give you an
example, one of the work

00:59:57.490 --> 01:00:00.190
that Google is doing is
to basically understand

01:00:00.190 --> 01:00:04.855
from images of the eye,
of your retina, whether--

01:00:04.855 --> 01:00:08.950
basically, they look at
your blood patterns there.

01:00:08.950 --> 01:00:11.410
And then actually they
can infer whether you

01:00:11.410 --> 01:00:13.849
have certain kinds of diabetes.

01:00:13.849 --> 01:00:15.640
Now, this is transformative,
because if you

01:00:15.640 --> 01:00:17.590
can do this for real--
and this is actually

01:00:17.590 --> 01:00:21.100
still very experimental and it
still has to be a lot verified.

01:00:21.100 --> 01:00:24.430
But if you can start doing this
with a camera on a smartphone,

01:00:24.430 --> 01:00:27.160
that means that
somebody that is let's

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:29.290
say in a poor
region of the world

01:00:29.290 --> 01:00:35.650
and hasn't access to super good
health systems and doctors,

01:00:35.650 --> 01:00:38.410
would just going to be able
to point his phone at his eye

01:00:38.410 --> 01:00:41.330
and know something very, very
important about his health.

01:00:41.330 --> 01:00:43.540
It also, of course, actually
even in rich countries

01:00:43.540 --> 01:00:45.040
where we have access
to health care,

01:00:45.040 --> 01:00:46.760
it means actually much
easier prevention.

01:00:46.760 --> 01:00:49.270
I think that that's going
to change a lot of things.

01:00:49.270 --> 01:00:51.070
Now, going back to the
problem, again, you

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:53.920
can do this if you can
actually build systems

01:00:53.920 --> 01:00:59.380
that are able to very often
understand the minute, very

01:00:59.380 --> 01:01:02.875
subtle kind of patterns
inside images, data

01:01:02.875 --> 01:01:06.390
set, temporal streams, whatnot.

01:01:06.390 --> 01:01:08.890
So that's actually
where very often you

01:01:08.890 --> 01:01:12.010
need to do very extensive
machine learning

01:01:12.010 --> 01:01:14.200
on very large data sets,
because in order for you

01:01:14.200 --> 01:01:16.150
to detect those
patterns you have

01:01:16.150 --> 01:01:20.300
to be able to identify those
patterns into very large data

01:01:20.300 --> 01:01:20.800
sets.

01:01:20.800 --> 01:01:21.490
And that's again--

01:01:21.490 --> 01:01:24.114
SETH SHOSTAK: So this brings to
mind a couple of possibilities.

01:01:24.114 --> 01:01:28.800
One is in the diagnosis realm--

01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:31.875
I'm fortunate I live
here in California.

01:01:31.875 --> 01:01:34.000
And if I don't feel well,
I can go to my physician.

01:01:34.000 --> 01:01:37.540
And he's seen me for years and
since I walk into his office

01:01:37.540 --> 01:01:40.210
he already has made
some analysis of me,

01:01:40.210 --> 01:01:42.940
because he knows what I should
look like-- that kind of thing.

01:01:42.940 --> 01:01:45.087
But I think it's
already the case--

01:01:45.087 --> 01:01:47.170
I mean, there have been
some demos of this, right?

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:51.316
That if you let the AI do the
analysis or the diagnosis,

01:01:51.316 --> 01:01:52.940
that it might be a
little more accurate

01:01:52.940 --> 01:01:55.960
than the human physician
because it doesn't forget all

01:01:55.960 --> 01:01:59.260
those rare conditions that the
physician has because they've

01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:00.280
never seen them.

01:02:00.280 --> 01:02:01.990
So there is that.

01:02:01.990 --> 01:02:03.961
And then there's the
question of, indeed,

01:02:03.961 --> 01:02:06.460
if you're in a part of the world
where you don't have access

01:02:06.460 --> 01:02:09.040
to that kind of health care,
that maybe your phone, which

01:02:09.040 --> 01:02:11.350
allows you to maybe do
some of these measurements,

01:02:11.350 --> 01:02:14.050
send that to the AI, and
then it diagnoses you,

01:02:14.050 --> 01:02:16.517
and you get a better
level of health care.

01:02:16.517 --> 01:02:19.100
MASSIMO MASCARO: Again, I mean,
this is still very early times

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:22.410
and this is actually still very
much in the domain of research

01:02:22.410 --> 01:02:23.340
and science.

01:02:23.340 --> 01:02:26.100
But I think,
actually, this is one

01:02:26.100 --> 01:02:29.880
of the transformations that's
going to happen in health care.

01:02:29.880 --> 01:02:32.410
I do agree with you that very
often actually today health

01:02:32.410 --> 01:02:32.910
care--

01:02:32.910 --> 01:02:34.920
I mean, while we have
wonderful doctors

01:02:34.920 --> 01:02:37.230
and the health care
science actually

01:02:37.230 --> 01:02:40.050
has improved as we probably
could never have dreamed,

01:02:40.050 --> 01:02:43.009
even like 20, 30 years ago--

01:02:43.009 --> 01:02:44.550
we know that there
are actually a lot

01:02:44.550 --> 01:02:46.560
of differences between doctors.

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:49.370
I mean, and the fact that
the doctor is a human person,

01:02:49.370 --> 01:02:51.090
a human being, is a factor.

01:02:51.090 --> 01:02:55.500
I mean, your doctor may have
himself a health issue the day

01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:56.910
before he's visiting you.

01:02:56.910 --> 01:03:01.680
And maybe he's not actually
completely at his best.

01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:03.450
Or he just actually
may have like a worry,

01:03:03.450 --> 01:03:06.112
because I don't know--

01:03:06.112 --> 01:03:08.070
had an argument with his
son actually the night

01:03:08.070 --> 01:03:09.822
before visiting you.

01:03:09.822 --> 01:03:11.780
And again, that's one
issue with humans, right?

01:03:11.780 --> 01:03:13.600
SETH SHOSTAK: Will they
have a good bedside manner?

01:03:13.600 --> 01:03:15.750
I mean, that's a question you
probably get all the time.

01:03:15.750 --> 01:03:17.791
I like my doctor because
he jokes around with me.

01:03:17.791 --> 01:03:18.822
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yes.

01:03:18.822 --> 01:03:20.280
But that's actually
what I'm saying

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:24.830
is that machine learning is
completely struck from this.

01:03:24.830 --> 01:03:26.580
If you have a machine
learning that works,

01:03:26.580 --> 01:03:29.340
it will work in a
very consistent way.

01:03:29.340 --> 01:03:31.260
So that's primary, but
of course, actually we

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:35.550
don't have yet holistically
machine learning

01:03:35.550 --> 01:03:40.407
systems that can even get
close to match current doctors.

01:03:40.407 --> 01:03:42.240
But that actually is
where a lot of research

01:03:42.240 --> 01:03:45.060
is going right now, because
it is going to transform

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:45.960
completely the field.

01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:48.480
Now, going back to science,
I mean, the path ahead

01:03:48.480 --> 01:03:52.500
of the doctor giving you a
full diagnosis being driven

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:56.910
by an AI, I think it is
actually doing a lot of research

01:03:56.910 --> 01:03:59.340
into applying AI to medicine.

01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:01.286
And it just goes
into the discussion

01:04:01.286 --> 01:04:02.410
we're having about science.

01:04:02.410 --> 01:04:05.220
Because those AIs
always come from you

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:07.920
having churned
through a lot of data

01:04:07.920 --> 01:04:12.750
and having matched whatever the
AIs, in this case predicting

01:04:12.750 --> 01:04:16.330
or diagnosing, with
what you know is true.

01:04:16.330 --> 01:04:19.950
And actually this requires a lot
of work in actual basic science

01:04:19.950 --> 01:04:20.980
to start with.

01:04:20.980 --> 01:04:23.480
SETH SHOSTAK: We're getting
some questions from the viewers.

01:04:23.480 --> 01:04:24.660
And here's one.

01:04:24.660 --> 01:04:27.030
Could you explain the
usage of the Google Cloud

01:04:27.030 --> 01:04:30.780
tools in the context of the
NASA Frontier Development Lab

01:04:30.780 --> 01:04:36.000
for its challenges, which are
in the fields of exo and astro?

01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:38.550
So you might want to explain
what exo and astro are.

01:04:38.550 --> 01:04:41.050
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah, so those
were the two things actually

01:04:41.050 --> 01:04:42.150
we touched on.

01:04:42.150 --> 01:04:45.780
So exo is our exoplanet
search work with FDL

01:04:45.780 --> 01:04:50.670
where we were basically trying
to train machine learning

01:04:50.670 --> 01:04:55.260
algorithms that would detect
exoplanet passage into the data

01:04:55.260 --> 01:04:58.980
from Kepler and then TESS.

01:04:58.980 --> 01:05:01.530
And astro [INAUDIBLE]
the astrobiology work.

01:05:01.530 --> 01:05:03.570
It really was two
streams of work.

01:05:03.570 --> 01:05:06.990
One stream was about simulating
these metabolic pathways

01:05:06.990 --> 01:05:09.390
to understand what,
again, life could

01:05:09.390 --> 01:05:11.310
look like on these exoplanets.

01:05:11.310 --> 01:05:13.230
And then the second
part was simulating

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:17.610
how those metabolic pathways
would affect the spectrum

01:05:17.610 --> 01:05:20.670
that you would-- spectrum is
basically the different colors

01:05:20.670 --> 01:05:23.160
that you basically see, the
different frequencies of light

01:05:23.160 --> 01:05:25.810
that you see actually as light
passes through the atmosphere.

01:05:25.810 --> 01:05:28.009
And when you actually
have that phenomenon,

01:05:28.009 --> 01:05:29.925
you can actually detect
what kind of chemicals

01:05:29.925 --> 01:05:31.380
are inside the atmosphere.

01:05:31.380 --> 01:05:34.110
Of course, there is a lot that
goes on inside this process

01:05:34.110 --> 01:05:38.420
and so what is in the atmosphere
doesn't correlate one to one,

01:05:38.420 --> 01:05:40.560
and what the spectrum look like.

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:42.690
And so the second
part was really

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:45.780
characterizing this
kind of light signature

01:05:45.780 --> 01:05:47.910
as basically a
spectrum of planets.

01:05:47.910 --> 01:05:53.910
So going to the
question, the tools

01:05:53.910 --> 01:05:57.665
actually that we use actually
were very wide-spanning.

01:05:57.665 --> 01:06:02.310
So one side, as I said, we
had actually all the data

01:06:02.310 --> 01:06:04.050
coming into Google Cloud.

01:06:04.050 --> 01:06:05.650
We actually spun a
lot of computation.

01:06:05.650 --> 01:06:08.520
So a lot of the simulations have
been run inside Google Cloud.

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:12.300
We took some very, very
sophisticated codes

01:06:12.300 --> 01:06:14.690
that actually NASA
had developed for,

01:06:14.690 --> 01:06:18.330
for example,
simulating atmospheres.

01:06:18.330 --> 01:06:22.110
And we basically adapted
them to run on Google Cloud.

01:06:22.110 --> 01:06:24.420
And then we run them as
very, very large scales.

01:06:24.420 --> 01:06:27.210
Then the second part is that
those machine learning modules

01:06:27.210 --> 01:06:31.925
actually have to be built. So
on the machine learning side,

01:06:31.925 --> 01:06:33.300
a lot of the
researchers actually

01:06:33.300 --> 01:06:36.540
use extensively something
that is becoming very, very

01:06:36.540 --> 01:06:40.410
popular in data science
and in general ML research

01:06:40.410 --> 01:06:42.600
is the ability to
of write notebooks.

01:06:42.600 --> 01:06:45.570
Now, for those of you
who are not technical,

01:06:45.570 --> 01:06:47.160
a notebook is
basically a document

01:06:47.160 --> 01:06:49.680
where you can basically
put code and execute in it.

01:06:49.680 --> 01:06:52.410
And you can actually
have graphics being

01:06:52.410 --> 01:06:54.580
done inside that document.

01:06:54.580 --> 01:06:57.150
So we had actually a system
that would allow our researchers

01:06:57.150 --> 01:07:00.450
to spawn their notebooks
inside our infrastructure

01:07:00.450 --> 01:07:04.800
and then run machine
learning through that.

01:07:04.800 --> 01:07:08.640
Also, Google actually
provides a number of tools

01:07:08.640 --> 01:07:11.160
to do what is called
serverless training.

01:07:11.160 --> 01:07:14.130
That is, you can basically
submit a piece of code

01:07:14.130 --> 01:07:16.230
to Google Cloud
and basically say

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:18.650
this is my algorithms
that I need to train

01:07:18.650 --> 01:07:20.520
and that's actually
where the data is.

01:07:20.520 --> 01:07:24.060
And this service that's called
Machine Learning Engine, ML

01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:27.270
Engine, from France, will
basically take this code

01:07:27.270 --> 01:07:28.810
and ultimately
train it by actually

01:07:28.810 --> 01:07:30.810
spawning the necessary
machines on the back-end.

01:07:30.810 --> 01:07:32.160
So this was very useful,
because again, you

01:07:32.160 --> 01:07:34.440
don't have to care about
how many machine I have here

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:36.720
or there, and actually
spawning up the machines

01:07:36.720 --> 01:07:38.610
with the right environments.

01:07:38.610 --> 01:07:40.950
So these actually were
the main basic tools.

01:07:40.950 --> 01:07:44.520
Now of course, as part of this,
those researchers-- and this

01:07:44.520 --> 01:07:47.010
is actually one thing I care
a lot in terms of people

01:07:47.010 --> 01:07:50.160
understanding-- they
loaded into Google Cloud

01:07:50.160 --> 01:07:53.280
a lot of open-source code
and scientific libraries

01:07:53.280 --> 01:07:55.051
that they found
useful for their work.

01:07:55.051 --> 01:07:56.550
And this actually
is the cool thing.

01:07:56.550 --> 01:07:58.010
I mean, you don't have
to think about, OK, I

01:07:58.010 --> 01:07:59.670
have to do this work
on Google Cloud,

01:07:59.670 --> 01:08:03.400
so I'm going to let go all the
tools that I'm familiar with

01:08:03.400 --> 01:08:05.430
and I'll get to learn
all these other tools.

01:08:05.430 --> 01:08:08.700
And maybe Google Cloud is
not made for astrophysics

01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:09.900
or chemical research.

01:08:09.900 --> 01:08:11.400
SETH SHOSTAK: It doesn't
involve a complete reset

01:08:11.400 --> 01:08:12.310
of your career, is that
what you're saying?

01:08:12.310 --> 01:08:14.100
MASSIMO MASCARO:
It does not at all.

01:08:14.100 --> 01:08:16.516
SETH SHOSTAK: Then let me ask
you because obviously people

01:08:16.516 --> 01:08:18.660
want to know, OK,
this sounds great,

01:08:18.660 --> 01:08:20.460
can you point to some
concrete results?

01:08:20.460 --> 01:08:24.359
I mean, has the Google
Cloud published a paper?

01:08:24.359 --> 01:08:26.310
Is the Nobel Prize
committee calling up?

01:08:26.310 --> 01:08:28.950
I mean, maybe the cloud is
going to get to go to Sweden.

01:08:28.950 --> 01:08:30.120
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah,
so first of all, I mean,

01:08:30.120 --> 01:08:32.430
one thing actually I want
to make sure it's clear

01:08:32.430 --> 01:08:34.890
is that these were actually
researchers working

01:08:34.890 --> 01:08:38.700
for this program, and they're
actual researchers that work

01:08:38.700 --> 01:08:39.899
very often in universities.

01:08:39.899 --> 01:08:44.100
They're usually postgraduate
students or postdocs

01:08:44.100 --> 01:08:46.950
or some actually assistant
professor that was mentoring.

01:08:46.950 --> 01:08:50.970
So as Google Cloud, we're
not going to publish a paper.

01:08:50.970 --> 01:08:52.770
Those researchers
will be and there

01:08:52.770 --> 01:08:55.439
are few actually that
are coming down the line.

01:08:55.439 --> 01:08:56.890
In terms of concrete
results, I'll

01:08:56.890 --> 01:08:58.330
mention a couple of things.

01:08:58.330 --> 01:09:02.810
The first one is that on the
exoplanet search research

01:09:02.810 --> 01:09:05.580
the team actually was
able to train a model

01:09:05.580 --> 01:09:10.290
to recognize those planets
inside the Kepler data sets.

01:09:10.290 --> 01:09:13.229
And then actually we simulated
that data set for tests.

01:09:13.229 --> 01:09:14.770
So again, as I said
at the beginning,

01:09:14.770 --> 01:09:18.250
we don't have yet the TESS data,
so we couldn't run it there.

01:09:18.250 --> 01:09:19.859
But we simulated a
data set from TESS.

01:09:19.859 --> 01:09:22.350
NASA has a very, very
sophisticated simulation

01:09:22.350 --> 01:09:24.330
of what TESS data
would look like.

01:09:24.330 --> 01:09:28.170
And they were able to train a
model on that, that on one side

01:09:28.170 --> 01:09:32.729
is more accurate at least in
percentages than what we know

01:09:32.729 --> 01:09:36.120
are the accuracies from the
previous models that have been

01:09:36.120 --> 01:09:38.760
attempted, including
the one from Google AI

01:09:38.760 --> 01:09:41.310
that I was mentioning before,
and including actually

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:44.160
the human performance that
is currently estimated.

01:09:44.160 --> 01:09:46.869
That is, astronomer
looking at the data.

01:09:46.869 --> 01:09:48.160
So the model was more accurate.

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:50.609
And I think, actually that
was, of course, a great result.

01:09:50.609 --> 01:09:52.770
But to me actually
even more exciting,

01:09:52.770 --> 01:09:56.370
the model ended up being a
couple of orders of magnitude

01:09:56.370 --> 01:09:59.250
smaller than the previous
attempts at models.

01:09:59.250 --> 01:10:00.700
That is, it's a
very small model.

01:10:00.700 --> 01:10:02.040
It's very compact.

01:10:02.040 --> 01:10:03.570
And what this allows
is now actually

01:10:03.570 --> 01:10:06.480
to consider running this
inline with data collection.

01:10:06.480 --> 01:10:09.150
Because before a lot of
those models actually

01:10:09.150 --> 01:10:13.300
were pretty heavy and they
required a lot of computation.

01:10:13.300 --> 01:10:16.260
So they couldn't have been
run as the data arrived.

01:10:16.260 --> 01:10:19.272
Being able to run a
model as data arrives

01:10:19.272 --> 01:10:20.730
opens up a number
of possibilities.

01:10:20.730 --> 01:10:22.780
For example, what
I mentioned before.

01:10:22.780 --> 01:10:25.110
If there is actually
a particular detection

01:10:25.110 --> 01:10:28.092
on TESS data as basically the
data set is getting extreme,

01:10:28.092 --> 01:10:29.550
it means actually
you can now alert

01:10:29.550 --> 01:10:31.620
other telescopes or
other researchers

01:10:31.620 --> 01:10:33.039
about that potential detection.

01:10:33.039 --> 01:10:34.830
And it means actually
collecting other data

01:10:34.830 --> 01:10:37.980
from other sources that
could confirm or deny

01:10:37.980 --> 01:10:41.237
that particular planet or maybe
extract other characteristics.

01:10:41.237 --> 01:10:43.320
One of the thing that I
mentioned in the beginning

01:10:43.320 --> 01:10:45.030
is that TESS actually
will only look

01:10:45.030 --> 01:10:47.480
at a patch of the
sky for 28 days.

01:10:47.480 --> 01:10:49.980
One limitation of this-- and
this actually is not completely

01:10:49.980 --> 01:10:52.271
true, because there is a
patch where they look actually

01:10:52.271 --> 01:10:53.230
for an entire year--

01:10:53.230 --> 01:10:56.040
but in those
patches you only get

01:10:56.040 --> 01:10:58.550
to see planets that have
very, very short orbits,

01:10:58.550 --> 01:11:00.120
because the planet
has to go around.

01:11:00.120 --> 01:11:00.990
SETH SHOSTAK: Inner planets.

01:11:00.990 --> 01:11:02.031
MASSIMO MASCARO: Exactly.

01:11:02.031 --> 01:11:05.140
And sometimes actually
you could see a transit,

01:11:05.140 --> 01:11:07.140
but then you don't get
to see the second transit

01:11:07.140 --> 01:11:09.205
as it comes around because
it's taking too long.

01:11:09.205 --> 01:11:11.580
But this actually could be
great candidates, for example,

01:11:11.580 --> 01:11:16.290
for NASA to now say, hey, we
have this potential candidates

01:11:16.290 --> 01:11:17.550
of planets.

01:11:17.550 --> 01:11:20.940
We don't know A, whether
they're real planets or not; B,

01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:24.050
how long actually it will
take to retransit again.

01:11:24.050 --> 01:11:25.805
And maybe we can
have, again, this

01:11:25.805 --> 01:11:28.410
is in science maybe
or other telescopes

01:11:28.410 --> 01:11:30.210
or other researchers,
they count on that

01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:32.670
and augment the TESS data sets.

01:11:32.670 --> 01:11:34.620
SETH SHOSTAK: Massi,
I mean, clearly this

01:11:34.620 --> 01:11:36.120
is kind of revolutionary.

01:11:36.120 --> 01:11:39.200
Maybe I should excise
the words kind of--

01:11:39.200 --> 01:11:40.110
it's revolutionary.

01:11:40.110 --> 01:11:43.410
I mean, it seems that we're
at the beginning where we've

01:11:43.410 --> 01:11:46.470
opened the door to a
whole new way of analyzing

01:11:46.470 --> 01:11:49.060
data and learning
things about the cosmos,

01:11:49.060 --> 01:11:51.450
but about nature in general.

01:11:51.450 --> 01:11:53.760
Have we reached a tipping point?

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:55.800
This is philosophical,
but you've

01:11:55.800 --> 01:11:57.930
figured that 500
years from now people

01:11:57.930 --> 01:12:00.570
will look back at the
beginning of the 21st century

01:12:00.570 --> 01:12:02.640
and say, the most important
thing that happened

01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:05.340
then was not reality television
or anything like that.

01:12:05.340 --> 01:12:06.880
[LAUGHTER]

01:12:06.880 --> 01:12:12.692
It was the beginning of AI
applied to real world problems.

01:12:12.692 --> 01:12:14.400
MASSIMO MASCARO: I
think, actually, we're

01:12:14.400 --> 01:12:18.770
at the tipping point where
this actually may happen.

01:12:18.770 --> 01:12:20.360
I could actually
tell you that, again,

01:12:20.360 --> 01:12:23.780
and I'm working in AI in a
much broader, if you want,

01:12:23.780 --> 01:12:26.840
spectrum of things
than science itself.

01:12:26.840 --> 01:12:30.230
AI is changing a lot
of the way in which we

01:12:30.230 --> 01:12:31.182
think about problems.

01:12:31.182 --> 01:12:32.640
In science, it's
one of the problem

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:33.860
that we as humans think of.

01:12:33.860 --> 01:12:35.693
It's probably one of
the most important one,

01:12:35.693 --> 01:12:39.440
because it's about understanding
our universe and ourselves.

01:12:39.440 --> 01:12:41.720
But AI is really kind
of at a tipping point

01:12:41.720 --> 01:12:43.700
where, if you want,
it's now starting

01:12:43.700 --> 01:12:46.850
to lead to results where
we can now solve problems

01:12:46.850 --> 01:12:49.230
in a very, very different way.

01:12:49.230 --> 01:12:51.020
Now, for scientists,
in particular,

01:12:51.020 --> 01:12:52.460
I think going back--

01:12:52.460 --> 01:12:55.010
I don't know in 500
years-- but going back we

01:12:55.010 --> 01:12:59.930
could really see that starting
from the '90s and then

01:12:59.930 --> 01:13:03.530
in the last two decades,
more and more, if you want,

01:13:03.530 --> 01:13:06.142
computers have been
applied to science.

01:13:06.142 --> 01:13:07.850
I mean, the initial
computers were almost

01:13:07.850 --> 01:13:09.470
developed for science.

01:13:09.470 --> 01:13:11.660
But now actually it's
becoming so prominent

01:13:11.660 --> 01:13:14.390
that the idea of doing
science without computers

01:13:14.390 --> 01:13:16.970
is completely
preposterous today.

01:13:16.970 --> 01:13:19.410
And I think, actually,
in a few decades,

01:13:19.410 --> 01:13:22.040
it may be that the idea of
doing science without AI

01:13:22.040 --> 01:13:25.347
would be as preposterous as
today is the idea of doing

01:13:25.347 --> 01:13:26.430
science without computers.

01:13:26.430 --> 01:13:28.179
SETH SHOSTAK: But what
about doing science

01:13:28.179 --> 01:13:29.154
without the scientists?

01:13:29.154 --> 01:13:30.320
I mean, how far can this go?

01:13:30.320 --> 01:13:31.720
MASSIMO MASCARO:
So this actually

01:13:31.720 --> 01:13:32.720
is an interesting point.

01:13:32.720 --> 01:13:34.345
I'll tell actually
an anecdote on this.

01:13:34.345 --> 01:13:37.337
So during one of
the final reviews--

01:13:37.337 --> 01:13:39.920
French technical reviews that
we had with Frontier Development

01:13:39.920 --> 01:13:42.110
Lab with FDL, who
were actually a number

01:13:42.110 --> 01:13:45.029
of labs-- of course, NASA
researchers and [INAUDIBLE],,

01:13:45.029 --> 01:13:46.820
I mean the teams actually
were showing some

01:13:46.820 --> 01:13:48.515
of those results made with AI.

01:13:48.515 --> 01:13:51.140
And there were actually a lot of
questions about, well, I mean,

01:13:51.140 --> 01:13:53.730
like if this AI is
predicting-- so for example,

01:13:53.730 --> 01:13:56.690
there was one study about
predicting solar activity

01:13:56.690 --> 01:13:59.060
and where they would actually
be able to predict much

01:13:59.060 --> 01:14:01.351
better than the physical
models that the scientists had

01:14:01.351 --> 01:14:03.370
basically created for that.

01:14:03.370 --> 01:14:05.330
And one question is
that, well, I mean,

01:14:05.330 --> 01:14:07.650
this thing is predicting
better than we are doing,

01:14:07.650 --> 01:14:10.420
but it's not really
explaining what is going on.

01:14:10.420 --> 01:14:12.620
At the end of the day, I
mean, in science, we want

01:14:12.620 --> 01:14:13.790
to understand the mechanism.

01:14:13.790 --> 01:14:17.040
We want to understand
the physics behind it.

01:14:17.040 --> 01:14:18.166
So I think, actually that--

01:14:18.166 --> 01:14:19.831
I mean, at least in
the spectrum of what

01:14:19.831 --> 01:14:21.500
we know today of
what the AI is doing,

01:14:21.500 --> 01:14:23.270
there is a lot of
space for scientists.

01:14:23.270 --> 01:14:26.750
Because eventually, these tools
will simulate a lot of data,

01:14:26.750 --> 01:14:29.030
will may actually be
able to detect patterns,

01:14:29.030 --> 01:14:33.260
may be able to refine
data sets in ways

01:14:33.260 --> 01:14:36.770
that we're not able to do
as scientists, as humans.

01:14:36.770 --> 01:14:38.830
But at the end of the
day, we also want to--

01:14:38.830 --> 01:14:42.140
I mean, science is really
about understanding the why--

01:14:42.140 --> 01:14:43.670
is that really
kind of going back

01:14:43.670 --> 01:14:46.460
to that kind of physical law
or like understanding actually

01:14:46.460 --> 01:14:47.270
the mechanism.

01:14:47.270 --> 01:14:49.520
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, you're
talking about insight here.

01:14:49.520 --> 01:14:52.020
I mean, Newton didn't
have AI working for him.

01:14:52.020 --> 01:14:53.600
He had his brain
and it turned out

01:14:53.600 --> 01:14:55.750
that was good enough
to understand many--

01:14:55.750 --> 01:14:58.840
but he didn't have
huge data sets.

01:14:58.840 --> 01:15:00.650
He had very limited data sets.

01:15:00.650 --> 01:15:03.920
Now, take a field such as,
well, you know a lot about.

01:15:03.920 --> 01:15:05.060
That's neuroscience.

01:15:05.060 --> 01:15:05.581
Right?

01:15:05.581 --> 01:15:07.580
They said the most complex
thing in the universe

01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:09.120
is the human brain.

01:15:09.120 --> 01:15:11.150
This may be an insult
to the Klingons, but--

01:15:11.150 --> 01:15:12.600
[LAUGHTER]

01:15:12.600 --> 01:15:13.310
--who's to say.

01:15:13.310 --> 01:15:16.790
And I suppose that at the
very small level where

01:15:16.790 --> 01:15:20.954
you have individual neurons and
so forth, and synapses and all

01:15:20.954 --> 01:15:23.120
that stuff, that you might
understand the chemistry,

01:15:23.120 --> 01:15:25.890
you might understand the physics
of what's going on there.

01:15:25.890 --> 01:15:28.910
But that doesn't mean that you
understand how thinking works,

01:15:28.910 --> 01:15:30.420
because that's very complex.

01:15:30.420 --> 01:15:32.660
This sounds like a
perfect application

01:15:32.660 --> 01:15:35.629
of this new technology,
but will it provide indeed

01:15:35.629 --> 01:15:37.670
the understanding of what
happens or does it just

01:15:37.670 --> 01:15:40.710
give you a model and you
just input a lot of data

01:15:40.710 --> 01:15:42.632
and now you can tell
what the next thought is.

01:15:42.632 --> 01:15:44.090
MASSIMO MASCARO:
Yeah, I mean, this

01:15:44.090 --> 01:15:45.180
goes to what I
was saying before.

01:15:45.180 --> 01:15:47.029
That is, right now
actually you could--

01:15:47.029 --> 01:15:49.070
I mean, there are applications
of AI, believe me,

01:15:49.070 --> 01:15:51.153
in neuroscience as well,
where they would actually

01:15:51.153 --> 01:15:53.450
start predicting
what neurons does

01:15:53.450 --> 01:15:56.270
or what extra group
of neurons does.

01:15:56.270 --> 01:15:59.670
However, it doesn't explain
necessarily how that works.

01:15:59.670 --> 01:16:04.350
So I think, again, it goes a
little bit into philosophy.

01:16:04.350 --> 01:16:06.500
It's a very important
thing today actually

01:16:06.500 --> 01:16:08.280
we don't get to do with AI.

01:16:08.280 --> 01:16:10.310
However, in the
context of brain,

01:16:10.310 --> 01:16:13.334
I think it is even more
if you want involved.

01:16:13.334 --> 01:16:14.750
Because in the
sense of what we're

01:16:14.750 --> 01:16:16.730
trying to do with AI
in a certain sense

01:16:16.730 --> 01:16:19.730
is to create artificial
intelligence.

01:16:19.730 --> 01:16:21.620
That's what the
word comes about.

01:16:21.620 --> 01:16:23.930
And one of the big
questions about the brain

01:16:23.930 --> 01:16:27.350
is, what is intelligence
and how does it work?

01:16:27.350 --> 01:16:30.650
If you can actually
create alternate versions

01:16:30.650 --> 01:16:33.020
of intelligence, they
may not have anything

01:16:33.020 --> 01:16:34.700
to do with how the brain works.

01:16:34.700 --> 01:16:36.200
It still gives you
a lot of insights

01:16:36.200 --> 01:16:38.985
of what intelligence could be
or how intelligence could work,

01:16:38.985 --> 01:16:41.660
or what are the limitations of
intelligence in certain areas

01:16:41.660 --> 01:16:42.376
or others.

01:16:42.376 --> 01:16:44.000
So I think, actually,
for neuroscience,

01:16:44.000 --> 01:16:46.580
I would say there
is the data part,

01:16:46.580 --> 01:16:48.290
there is the prediction
part, that is

01:16:48.290 --> 01:16:49.970
the same as with all sciences.

01:16:49.970 --> 01:16:51.680
But then the specific
problem that you

01:16:51.680 --> 01:16:54.080
solve when you've studied
neuroscience, or at least one

01:16:54.080 --> 01:16:56.840
of the specific
problems, it's very, very

01:16:56.840 --> 01:17:00.050
close to what the essence of
artificial intelligence is.

01:17:00.050 --> 01:17:03.060
SETH SHOSTAK: Wait, is
that like 100 years ago--

01:17:03.060 --> 01:17:04.070
a little more now--

01:17:04.070 --> 01:17:06.590
when they were trying to
build flying machines,

01:17:06.590 --> 01:17:08.720
they tried at first
to imitate birds.

01:17:08.720 --> 01:17:10.600
We'll have wings that
flap and so forth.

01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:12.844
And that turned out not
to be the right approach.

01:17:12.844 --> 01:17:15.260
They developed something where
the wings don't move at all

01:17:15.260 --> 01:17:16.930
and you just have a big
propeller in the front.

01:17:16.930 --> 01:17:18.054
I mean, that's not the way.

01:17:18.054 --> 01:17:21.095
Very few of the birds in my
backyard have propellers.

01:17:21.095 --> 01:17:22.470
And yet they could
make machines,

01:17:22.470 --> 01:17:24.610
they could fly pretty darn well.

01:17:24.610 --> 01:17:26.880
But in the end, that
actually gave them insights

01:17:26.880 --> 01:17:28.350
into how birds fly.

01:17:28.350 --> 01:17:29.942
So are you talking
about something

01:17:29.942 --> 01:17:31.650
like that, where you
build a machine that

01:17:31.650 --> 01:17:33.804
can do some thinking
process, but it

01:17:33.804 --> 01:17:35.470
doesn't work at all
like the human brain

01:17:35.470 --> 01:17:37.277
but that might be helpful?

01:17:37.277 --> 01:17:39.360
MASSIMO MASCARO: So if you
want like a jet engine,

01:17:39.360 --> 01:17:42.390
it's not very similar
to a bird even if it has

01:17:42.390 --> 01:17:44.660
some kind of characteristics.

01:17:44.660 --> 01:17:47.070
And a helicopter is complete
different than a bird.

01:17:47.070 --> 01:17:51.720
However, the principle
that make things fly apply.

01:17:51.720 --> 01:17:53.280
And in a sense,
actually if you want,

01:17:53.280 --> 01:17:57.510
if you go to the very kind
of bedrock of what flying is,

01:17:57.510 --> 01:17:59.970
of how we generate
lift, you can actually

01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:00.960
learn a lot from birds.

01:18:00.960 --> 01:18:03.168
But you can learn also a
lot from helicopters and jet

01:18:03.168 --> 01:18:03.840
engines.

01:18:03.840 --> 01:18:05.520
So the idea here
is that our brain

01:18:05.520 --> 01:18:07.840
is a way of doing
intelligence, and if you

01:18:07.840 --> 01:18:11.070
build another way of
doing intelligence maybe

01:18:11.070 --> 01:18:14.910
by completely different means,
that may actually still give

01:18:14.910 --> 01:18:17.820
you insights about the bedrock
of what intelligence is

01:18:17.820 --> 01:18:19.030
and how intelligence works.

01:18:19.030 --> 01:18:22.380
Now that said, I would
actually just for the public

01:18:22.380 --> 01:18:26.250
say that the most successful
way of doing AI today

01:18:26.250 --> 01:18:27.440
is deep learning.

01:18:27.440 --> 01:18:29.580
And it's based on this
idea of neural networks.

01:18:29.580 --> 01:18:32.040
That a lot of people
that as me actually

01:18:32.040 --> 01:18:34.170
have studied computation
neuroscience will say,

01:18:34.170 --> 01:18:36.420
oh, neural networks are very
different from the brain.

01:18:36.420 --> 01:18:36.980
And it's true.

01:18:36.980 --> 01:18:38.855
They are very, very
different from the brain.

01:18:38.855 --> 01:18:41.280
However, they have
a lot of analogies.

01:18:41.280 --> 01:18:44.430
And those analogies actually
may give us a hint about, again,

01:18:44.430 --> 01:18:47.490
that bedrock, about what
intelligence is and how

01:18:47.490 --> 01:18:49.267
actually you can build
systems that learn,

01:18:49.267 --> 01:18:50.850
because at the end
of the day, I mean,

01:18:50.850 --> 01:18:54.150
intelligence is a
very fuzzy word.

01:18:54.150 --> 01:18:56.910
But learning is something that
we are much more familiar with.

01:18:56.910 --> 01:18:59.326
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, I mean,
Google did this experiment in,

01:18:59.326 --> 01:18:59.850
what, 2012?

01:18:59.850 --> 01:19:00.880
I don't quite remember.

01:19:00.880 --> 01:19:03.840
But they took 16,000 processors,
whatever it was, and they

01:19:03.840 --> 01:19:06.300
let it loose on the internet
and said, go find something,

01:19:06.300 --> 01:19:10.470
and it found cats, with very
high accuracy by the way.

01:19:10.470 --> 01:19:12.210
I mean it found
cats probably better

01:19:12.210 --> 01:19:14.060
than any cat could find cat.

01:19:14.060 --> 01:19:14.814
I don't know.

01:19:14.814 --> 01:19:16.730
And you can say, well,
that's not very useful.

01:19:16.730 --> 01:19:18.930
But of course, the fact
that it could see things

01:19:18.930 --> 01:19:19.630
on the internet.

01:19:19.630 --> 01:19:20.940
I mean, so that's--

01:19:20.940 --> 01:19:23.110
I guess that's machine learning.

01:19:23.110 --> 01:19:27.694
But I think a lot of people
regard all these capabilities--

01:19:27.694 --> 01:19:29.610
the fact that if you're
a Go player or a poker

01:19:29.610 --> 01:19:31.560
player or a chess
player, there's

01:19:31.560 --> 01:19:33.300
a machine that can beat you.

01:19:33.300 --> 01:19:35.164
They regard them as a threat.

01:19:35.164 --> 01:19:36.830
And should we be
looking at it that way?

01:19:36.830 --> 01:19:38.310
AI, is that going
to take my job?

01:19:38.310 --> 01:19:40.300
Is it going to
destroy my lifestyle?

01:19:40.300 --> 01:19:41.370
What's your view?

01:19:41.370 --> 01:19:43.621
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah, so
my personal view on this

01:19:43.621 --> 01:19:45.870
is that, I think, actually
this is one of the greatest

01:19:45.870 --> 01:19:48.130
opportunity of humanity.

01:19:48.130 --> 01:19:51.900
So I see it actually as
with a very optimistic take.

01:19:51.900 --> 01:19:53.430
I wouldn't work
on it, if I wasn't

01:19:53.430 --> 01:19:54.630
very optimistic about it.

01:19:54.630 --> 01:19:58.500
That said, I think, actually AI
does have dangers and does have

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:03.480
questions that we have to solve
both in applicative ways--

01:20:03.480 --> 01:20:06.540
so what are the constraints
where you want to apply AI--

01:20:06.540 --> 01:20:08.730
and also from a
society perspective.

01:20:08.730 --> 01:20:14.940
One thing we did at Google
is that we very recently

01:20:14.940 --> 01:20:16.380
this summer came out for--

01:20:16.380 --> 01:20:18.330
we're the first company
to really do that--

01:20:18.330 --> 01:20:20.370
come up with AI principles.

01:20:20.370 --> 01:20:22.620
So we have actually
a code of conduct

01:20:22.620 --> 01:20:25.680
in terms of where we
think AI could be applied

01:20:25.680 --> 01:20:27.780
and where it shouldn't,
and how it should

01:20:27.780 --> 01:20:30.270
be applied when it's there.

01:20:30.270 --> 01:20:35.100
Now, I personally don't think
that this kind of debate

01:20:35.100 --> 01:20:38.550
can only be in the realm of
a corporation like Google.

01:20:38.550 --> 01:20:42.900
Google, of course, has a lot
of investment and trust in AI,

01:20:42.900 --> 01:20:44.521
but it's not the
only player here.

01:20:44.521 --> 01:20:46.020
And I think, actually
we should have

01:20:46.020 --> 01:20:48.450
more of a societal
discussion about this.

01:20:48.450 --> 01:20:50.116
SETH SHOSTAK: Is that
realistic, though?

01:20:50.116 --> 01:20:52.110
I mean, 99% of the
world agrees, yes,

01:20:52.110 --> 01:20:54.790
we have to have these
boundaries-- whatever--

01:20:54.790 --> 01:20:57.570
but the 1% that doesn't believe
in that or sees some advantage

01:20:57.570 --> 01:20:58.950
to themselves--

01:20:58.950 --> 01:21:01.050
it sounds like there
might be some--

01:21:01.050 --> 01:21:03.930
MASSIMO MASCARO: As a
prominent researcher

01:21:03.930 --> 01:21:08.640
Nick Bostrom pointed out, AI
is intrinsically adversarial.

01:21:08.640 --> 01:21:10.694
Because to your point,
it just takes 1%

01:21:10.694 --> 01:21:12.360
to do the things that
they shouldn't do.

01:21:12.360 --> 01:21:14.850
In a sense, this is not very
dissimilar to other kind

01:21:14.850 --> 01:21:17.380
of dangerous things that
we do, from nanotechnology

01:21:17.380 --> 01:21:19.289
to biotechnology and
things like those.

01:21:19.289 --> 01:21:21.330
But that's actually is
why it's important to have

01:21:21.330 --> 01:21:22.410
that societal discussion.

01:21:22.410 --> 01:21:24.540
Because I don't think,
actually any corporation,

01:21:24.540 --> 01:21:27.150
any institution
itself, on his own

01:21:27.150 --> 01:21:31.650
actually can really put
the right boundaries.

01:21:31.650 --> 01:21:32.540
I think it's really--

01:21:32.540 --> 01:21:35.370
it's becoming more and
more a humanity discussion.

01:21:35.370 --> 01:21:38.530
And it's interesting because we
are in a point of our history,

01:21:38.530 --> 01:21:41.190
of our world, where it
seems actually we're

01:21:41.190 --> 01:21:44.010
getting more fractured
instead of getting more united

01:21:44.010 --> 01:21:46.420
as a global humanity.

01:21:46.420 --> 01:21:48.480
And I see actual danger in that.

01:21:48.480 --> 01:21:51.120
On the other side, this could be
things actually that unite us.

01:21:51.120 --> 01:21:52.911
Because to your point,
I think the majority

01:21:52.911 --> 01:21:54.360
of the people on
Earth would agree

01:21:54.360 --> 01:21:56.741
on a number of those things.

01:21:56.741 --> 01:21:58.740
SETH SHOSTAK: Well,
finally, then Massi, I mean,

01:21:58.740 --> 01:22:00.960
it sounds like we're at a
stage in the development

01:22:00.960 --> 01:22:02.190
of technology here--

01:22:02.190 --> 01:22:04.500
kind of analogous to the
invention of the microscope,

01:22:04.500 --> 01:22:05.640
the telescope.

01:22:05.640 --> 01:22:08.400
These were tools that
allowed us to suddenly make

01:22:08.400 --> 01:22:10.500
big discoveries.

01:22:10.500 --> 01:22:12.900
It sounds like you're saying
that the 21st century--

01:22:12.900 --> 01:22:14.880
the first half of
the 21st century--

01:22:14.880 --> 01:22:18.510
we might see not only the
detection of lots of planets,

01:22:18.510 --> 01:22:21.780
but life on those planets,
maybe even intelligent life

01:22:21.780 --> 01:22:22.620
on those planets.

01:22:22.620 --> 01:22:24.870
And in medicine,
we might be curing

01:22:24.870 --> 01:22:27.390
all sorts of diseases
that have stymied us

01:22:27.390 --> 01:22:29.100
for hundreds of years.

01:22:29.100 --> 01:22:31.710
And you wouldn't want
to rule out the tools.

01:22:31.710 --> 01:22:33.690
I mean, you want to
embrace those tools.

01:22:33.690 --> 01:22:35.820
What's your advice
to young people

01:22:35.820 --> 01:22:38.640
who are getting interested
in science and technology?

01:22:38.640 --> 01:22:40.150
MASSIMO MASCARO:
So again, I think,

01:22:40.150 --> 01:22:43.050
actually it's an amazing time.

01:22:43.050 --> 01:22:46.320
I've always been very passionate
about science and technology.

01:22:46.320 --> 01:22:49.530
And when I was young,
it used to be that--

01:22:49.530 --> 01:22:51.540
I mean, unless you
were really lucky to go

01:22:51.540 --> 01:22:53.490
to a good university
and you have access

01:22:53.490 --> 01:22:56.040
to libraries and
journals, you can't really

01:22:56.040 --> 01:22:57.150
learn a lot of things.

01:22:57.150 --> 01:23:00.930
Nowadays, I mean, young
people can go on YouTube

01:23:00.930 --> 01:23:04.770
and watch lessons of the
best professors in the best

01:23:04.770 --> 01:23:07.750
universities for free.

01:23:07.750 --> 01:23:09.570
And this actually
is great content.

01:23:09.570 --> 01:23:11.860
You guys can play with
things, as I said.

01:23:11.860 --> 01:23:13.410
It doesn't take a lot of money.

01:23:13.410 --> 01:23:15.909
And by the way, I mean, there
are also educational programs.

01:23:15.909 --> 01:23:17.790
If you guys are
interested, you can ping us

01:23:17.790 --> 01:23:20.527
at Google Cloud, where we can
actually give you credits.

01:23:20.527 --> 01:23:22.110
But what I'm saying
is that it doesn't

01:23:22.110 --> 01:23:25.315
take much to get and even
do serious science if you

01:23:25.315 --> 01:23:26.190
are interested in it.

01:23:26.190 --> 01:23:28.410
And I think, this actually
democratizes science

01:23:28.410 --> 01:23:28.944
enormously.

01:23:28.944 --> 01:23:31.110
And I'm really hoping that
for the young people that

01:23:31.110 --> 01:23:33.600
are listening to us,
that they'll get actually

01:23:33.600 --> 01:23:36.300
the passion for science
in the right way.

01:23:36.300 --> 01:23:38.400
And now they have all
the tools literally

01:23:38.400 --> 01:23:42.624
inside their laptop to
go and do seriously it.

01:23:42.624 --> 01:23:44.290
And I think, actually
this is the point.

01:23:44.290 --> 01:23:46.980
This actually enlarges
the targeted population

01:23:46.980 --> 01:23:49.830
for scientists by a number
of order of magnitude

01:23:49.830 --> 01:23:51.437
from what it used to be.

01:23:51.437 --> 01:23:53.520
SETH SHOSTAK: It sounds
like we're opening doors--

01:23:53.520 --> 01:23:54.960
big doors--

01:23:54.960 --> 01:23:58.330
I think people will have the
motivation to walk through.

01:23:58.330 --> 01:24:01.590
Massimo Mascaro, thanks so
very much for speaking with us.

01:24:01.590 --> 01:24:02.540
I'm Seth Shostak.

01:24:02.540 --> 01:24:04.110
Thank you for tuning in.

01:24:04.110 --> 01:24:07.860
And we'll see you in another
Google Facebook Live.

01:24:07.860 --> 01:24:09.620
MASSIMO MASCARO: Thank you.

